  "body_html": "<h1>Social Choice Function</h1>\n<p>A <em>social choice function</em> (SCF) on a population $N$ and alternative set $A$\nis a map\n$$\nG : \\mathrm{PrefProfile}(N, A) \\to A\n$$\nthat selects, for every preference profile, a single winning alternative.</p>\n<p>In Lean this is the structure <code>SocialChoice.Voting.SCF N A</code> with one field\n<code>f : PrefProfile N A → A</code>, equipped with a <code>CoeFun</code> so <code>G P</code> is the chosen\nalternative at profile $P$.</p>\n<p>Compared with a social welfare function ([[social_choice.voting.swf]]) the\nSCF returns only the top-pick rather than a full ranking. The standard\naxioms — unanimity ([[social_choice.voting.unanimity]]), monotonicity\n([[social_choice.voting.monotonic_scf]]), strategy-proofness\n([[social_choice.voting.strategyproof]]), and (non-)dictatorship\n([[social_choice.voting.dictatorial_scf]]) — are all stated directly on\n$G(P)$.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MSZ 21.21] Maschler, Solan, and Zamir, <em>Game Theory</em>, <code>references/GameTheory.pdf</code>. Social choice function.</li>\n</ul>",
